<!--
    A global read back after a store through a pointer that may point at it
    (P3 heritage, option indexaliasguard global; GitHub issue #792).

        int gi;
        int s_param(int a, int *p, int k) { gi = a; *p = k; return gi / 16; }

    The binary loads gi again after `*p = k`.  At `indexaliasguard load` a
    pointer STORE has no effect on a global in kuna's SSA, so that load read
    the COPY of `a` and printed `a0`: with p == &gi the rebuilt function
    returns 0 where the binary returns -4.  The default `global` level puts an
    INDIRECT on the global at every STORE into its space, so the load reads
    the global, a value read before the store keeps its own variable instead
    of printing as a read after it, and the store to the global stays in front
    of the pointer store.  A value loaded through a pointer before a store to
    the global (r_order) keeps its own variable at the same level, and so does
    a value read from the global before the store and stored back after it
    (w_inc).  So does a value loaded through the pointer before a store to a
    global on a branch (r_cond), on two branches to two globals (r_two) or in
    a loop (r_loop): such a store reaches the global through a MULTIEQUAL, so
    the store's own output is a temporary merged into the global's variable.
    A store to the global stays in front of a load through the pointer that a
    call follows (e_call): moved into the call's INDIRECT, it printed after
    the load.  Both levels print a store the binary makes ahead of the load
    in front of it when a branch stores again (e_reg, w_dead, w_dead2) and
    when the -O0 build shares the return block with another path (w_epi);
    and w_big and w_big2, with more pointer stores than heritage guards, keep
    the `load` output for gk.

    Pass 1 (`load`) reproduces the defect, pass 2 (the default) the fix, both
    on storealias_gcc_O2_x86_64 (built from storealias_x86_64.c beside it);
    passes 3 and 4 do the same for r_loop, w_epi, w_big and w_big2 on
    storealias_gcc_O0_x86_64.
    The wrapper only supplies the harness architecture.
-->
